Z||000018||Formula88||07-03-2001||12:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierodude:<BR><B>ive got a 1977 black and gold edition t\a stoed for 12 years my father gave me the car when i was 7,ive had it ever since its got t-roofs,power everything and a 455ho stock,now as for the lepard skin seat covers thats what the fools added on that wannabe smokey and the bandit music vid, gee never knew burt reynolds was black or sheriff bubert t justice for that matter!!lol anyway my car is in mint show room condition with only 55,000km and id sell my fiero in a second rather than it! (:+<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ok, not trying to start anything, but the 455 wasn't available in 77.  Also, the last year of the 455HO was 72. In 75, they put a standard 455 in the TA and called it an HO, but the automotive press ripped Pontiac a new one for the misnomer, so in 76, they just called it a 455.  <P>The things that made the HO different were, nodular iron crank, 4 bolt mains, round port heads, aluminum 2pc intake, cast iron headers, and a 780-800cfm Q-jet.  These were the same heads as used on the old RAM AIR IV, but with lower compression.  The SD heads are also similar, but had much larger ports.  The 455 used in 75 and 76 was the same one that came in any station wagon.  Nothing different.||||reg||1
